{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787151838,"nonce":"Non9kYI+vOX/dQmq4rV427p8FL4=","message":"**Done, in `43ee219`** — with the two undetermined questions answered rather than left open, and with one correction to this ticket's own framing that is worth more than the fix.\n\n**⚠️ First, the correction: nothing produces `refired`.** This ticket says *\"`refired` is now **the only reason that broadcasts** … apart from the configurable `all_resolved`\"*, and that is true of `BroadcastPolicy.Warrants` but not of the running product. `git grep -nI 'ReasonRefired' -- '*.go'` outside tests returns eight lines and every one is vocabulary, classification or rendering — `reason.go:52,59,119,258,315`, `verbosity.go:52`, `broadcast.go:101`, `service/view.go:382`. Not one CONSTRUCTS a notification with it. `broadcast.go:71-83` says so in its own words: *\"⛔ RETAINED FOR HISTORY; NOTHING PRODUCES IT … ADR 0040 retired T8: every re-fire now opens a new episode.\"* So the message in the subject line cannot currently be produced, and with `all_resolved` defaulting to off, **oto broadcasts nothing at all out of the box today.** The defect was real; its subject named the wrong carrier. That is an argument FOR the fix being keyed on the mode, which is what (ii) below does.\n\n**(i) The link goes in the BODY, not the top-level `text`.** Three reasons, and the third is the one that settles it rather than merely favouring it:\n\n1. ⭐ **A button is impossible here, confirmed by observation — so a link is not the safest affordance, it is the only one.** ADR 0020 rule 5b is marked *\"⭐ CONFIRMED, not merely cautious\"*: a human watched the in-channel copy show no buttons beside a root card in the same channel showing all of them. A mrkdwn link is not a button and not an interactive element, which is what leaves it available.\n\n   ⛔ **I got this backwards first, and the ADR is why.** `17f8055`'s comment and commit message both said Amendment 4 *\"left unverified\"* the buttons. It says the opposite in three places — the evidence table (*\"cannot contain message buttons — ⭐ TRUE\"*), rule 5b itself, and \"What is still unknown\", which is **client parity**. The cause was the ADR's own summary callout at rule 5, which said 5b was *\"still unverified\"* 360 lines above the amendment confirming it. I read the summary, believed it, never reached the body. Both are corrected in `522bef5`, and the callout now records why a summary that disagrees with what it summarises is worse than none. Caught by a review lens, not by me.\n\n   ⚠️ **And the corrected reading weakens a premise I have to state rather than bury.** Amendment 4's three data points are: the attachment comes back from `conversations.history`, the colour bar renders, the buttons do not. **None of them watched a section block's TEXT render in the channel copy**, and Amendment 4 warns in as many words that *\"`blocks` being present in the stored attachment is storage, not rendering\"*. So \"a link in the body reaches a channel reader\" is INFERRED. It is a strong inference — a copy rendering no blocks would have been reported as blank rather than as button-less — and it is now an observation owed on `2078a07` (comment #2), with the one test that has to change if the answer is no.\n2. Rule 4 binds the top-level `text` to be a self-sufficient **sentence**, and that same string is the push notification on a locked phone and the text a screen reader announces. A URL is not a sentence, and a locked phone cannot follow one.\n3. ⭐ **The Block Kit Builder capture is `{\"blocks\": […]}` — the attachment's block list lifted out of the payload (`test/harness/slack_cards.go:426`). The top-level `text` is not in it.** So a link placed in the `text` would have left `broadcast_refired.blockkit.json` with no mrkdwn control character, and the per-card escaping guarantee this same ticket asks to restore could not have come back. Body placement is the only choice under which both halves of this ticket are satisfiable together — which is why reading the fixture, and not just the renderer, decided it.\n\n**(ii) Yes, `all_resolved` had the identical gap, and one line closes both.** The guard is `if o.Mode == domain.ModeBroadcastReply \u0026\u0026 v.Links.Group != \"\"`, keyed on the MODE and not on the reason, for the same reason `replyText` adds its facts clause to every reply and not only to the broadcasting ones: a rule that holds only for the reasons that happen to broadcast today breaks the first time the broadcast set changes. `degraded` and `continued` already carry a link and cannot be doubled up, because `Warrants` never returns true for either.\n\n**The escaping guarantee is back in its per-card form**, which is the stronger check and is what this ticket asked for. `bd0fb1d` weakened it to a corpus-wide assertion for an honest reason — it had just deleted the card that carried the only link, and the choice was to invent fixture content or drop the guarantee. This removes the premise instead: every capture now contains a `\u003c` on its own merits (1, 3, 6, 5, 5, 1 across the six).\n\n**The bytes were predicted before `-update-slack-goldens` ran**, so the goldens are provably right rather than merely green. The appended literal is 83 characters / 85 bytes — the em dash is three — so `wire_bytes` had to move 615 → 700. It did, exactly. The top-level `text` and the `fallback` did **not** move, which is what proves the link did not leak into the sentence; `blocks` stayed at 1; and the five non-broadcast cards did not change a byte, which is what proves the mode guard does what it claims.\n\n**Gate.** The eleven `ci` recipes run individually, four times over the night, each with `dirty_before 0` and `dirty_after 0` so every run provably measured a committed tree. Final tip `9761298`: nine green, and the two reds are exactly the two measured baselines — `lint-vocabulary` at exactly 60, byte-identical to pre-flight, and one `--- FAIL` in the whole suite, `TestNoStateHueInChrome`, pre-existing from `59c7def`. Zero drift from baseline in either direction.\n\n⛔ **The first gate on this work was RED and the summary could not see it.** Exit codes matched the baseline exactly — `test 1` either way — while `grep -c '^--- FAIL'` had gone from 1 to 2. The new one was `TestGoldenBroadcastRefired`: **there are two Slack golden corpora and they take different flags.** `test/fixtures/slack/**` is the Block Kit Builder artefact behind `-update-slack-goldens`; `internal/channels/render/slack/testdata/*.golden.json` is the renderer's own wire corpus behind a plain `-update`. They also carry different `Links.Group` fixtures, so the appended literal is 85 bytes in one and 84 in the other — both predicted, both exact (819→903 for the second). Comparing exit codes to a baseline is not comparing to a baseline; compare the failure names.\n\n**A red team then refuted two claims this work made about itself**, and both are fixed in `9761298`: the SPEC §L.5.1 block did NOT \"match the file\" after the `mention_on_reminder` deletion (`transport` still diverged three ways, now compared by PARSING both sides), and ADR 0020's Amendment 4 \"What does not change\" section was a twenty-second unmarked site asserting the deleted reminder is still in the broadcast set — which also falsified a sentence I had just written in Amendment 5.\n\n**Two follow-ups filed from findings on this work, neither in scope here:** `3f5e952` — `http.base_url` is unvalidated, and blanking it makes `Links.Group` empty so this fix's guard silently adds nothing, re-opening the defect; `1cd496f` — `truncateSection`'s *\"see full detail in oto\"* suffix costs `len(URL) + 29` against a fixed 100-byte allowance, so any oto deep link over **71 bytes** makes the function that enforces Slack's 3 000-character limit return more than 3 000. The shipped default leaves four bytes of headroom.\n\n⚠️ **Not pushed.** The four commits sit on branch `afk/20260819-1920`; no push authority was granted for this session, and the git-bug store needs its own `git-bug push origin` besides.","files":null}]}